```

Before you run it: create or open an R project folder so that it is your working directory, then save this file in that folder under the name you will upload, and check that autosave is on (*File → Auto Save*). See OnQ and use the positpal chatbot if you are having trouble with positpal.

# Part 1 — Import and inspect

The data file is `data/AbroniaFloralData.csv`: one row per plant, from 12 coastal sites, with the mating system of the site (`self_inc`: SI or SC), the site's latitude, and four floral traits. The instructions describe the study and the two hypotheses. **Do not edit the file by hand.**

## 1. Import the data

Import the observations and save it as an object called `floral_data`.

```{r import-data}

floral_data <- read.csv("./data/AbroniaFloralData.csv")

```

## 2. Inspect the data

Check the object with `dim()`, `head()`, `str()` and `summary()`. Look for the usual problems: columns of the wrong type, and missing values.

```{r inspect-data}

dim(floral_data)
head(floral_data)
str(floral_data)
summary(floral_data)

```

**Question 1:** In statistical terms, what is the **statistical population** in this study, and what is the **sample**? Give one reason the sample might not be a random, unbiased sample of that population.

**Answer: the statistical population in this study would be *Abronia umbellata* self-incompatible (SI) and self-compatible (SC), while the sample is were the current 966 flowers tested from north of Los Angeles California to northern range limit in southern Oregon in the data. One reason the sample might not be a random, unbiased sample of that population is the (more si than sc? p-value? mean and median too close?? **

# Part 2 — Distributions

## 3. Plot frequency distributions

Plot the distributions of `face_diam_mm`, with facets so that SI and SC plants are in separate panels. Choose an appropriate `binwidth` that shows the shape of the data distribution, add axis labels with units and a title, and use a 'clean' theme suitable for publication. Then do the same for `flower_number`, with `binwidth = 1`. If R prints a message about `bins = 30`, you have not set the binwidth; the chunk option `message=FALSE` (already set on these chunks) hides messages like that in the knitted file.

```{r face-histogram, message=FALSE}

library(ggplot2) # plotting library
source("http://bit.ly/theme_pub") # Set custom plotting themetheme_set(theme_pub())

face_diameter <- floral_data$face_diam_mm
ggplot(aes(x=face_diameter),data=floral_data) + geom_histogram(binwidth=0.5, colour="maroon4",fill="lightpink2") + facet_wrap(vars(self_inc)) + labs(title="Distribution of face diameter in self-incompatible and self-compatible Abronia umbellata", subtitle="distribution of SI and SC face diameter in 12 sites",caption="Figure 1: shows the distribution of face diameter in self-incompatible (SI) and self-compatible (SC) Nyctaginaceae in 12 sites from north of Los Angeles California to northern range limit in southern Oregon") + xlab("Face Diameter (mm)") + ylab("Flower Count") + theme_classic()

```

```{r flower-histogram, message=FALSE}

flower_number <- floral_data$flower_number
ggplot(aes(x=flower_number),data=floral_data) + geom_histogram(binwidth=1, colour="maroon4",fill="lightpink2") + facet_wrap(vars(self_inc)) + labs(title="Distribution of flower number in self-incompatible and self-compatible Abronia umbellata", subtitle="distribution of flower nu,ber in SI and SC in 12 sites",caption="Figure 2: shows the distribution of flower number in self-incompatible (SI) and self-compatible (SC) Nyctaginaceae in 12 sites from north of Los Angeles California to northern range limit in southern Oregon") + xlab("Flower Number") + ylab("Flower Count") + theme_classic()

```

**Question 2:** Of the three common population distributions (Gaussian, Poisson, binomial), which one is the best model for `face_diam_mm`, and which for `flower_number`? Explain with respect to the *type* of data as well as the shape of the histogram.

**Answer:**

## 4. Mean, standard deviation and coefficient of variation

Make two vectors, `SI` and `SC`, containing the face diameters of the SI plants and of the SC plants. For **each** vector, calculate the sample size, the mean, the standard deviation, and the coefficient of variation. 

```{r metrics}

library(tidyverse) # Loading Libraries 

SI <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SI") %>% summarise(mean=mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SD=sd(face_diam_mm, na.rm=TRUE), CV=SD/mean)
print(SI)

SC <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SC") %>% summarise(mean=mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SD=sd(face_diam_mm, na.rm=TRUE), CV=SD/mean)
print(SC)

```

**Question 3:** The two standard deviations are very different, but the two coefficients of variation are almost the same. What does that tell you about how variation in flower size relates to mean flower size?

**Answer:**

## 5. Trait means by mating system and by site

Report the sample size and the mean of each of the four traits for SI and for SC plants, using `dplyr` commands. One trait has missing values, so the code must handle `NA`.

```{r trait-means}
# SI umbel angle degree
SI_umbel_angle <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SI") %>% summarise(mean=mean(umbel_angle_deg, na.rm = TRUE))
N_SI_umbel <- sum(!is.na(floral_data$umbel_angle_deg))

# SI flower number
SI_flower_number <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SI") %>% summarise(mean=mean(flower_number, na.rm = TRUE))
N_SI_flowernumber <- sum(!is.na(floral_data$flower_number))

# SI face diameter (mm)
SI_face_diameter <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SI") %>% summarise(mean=mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE))
N_SI_face_diameter <- sum(!is.na(floral_data$face_diam_mm))

# SI tube lenght (mm)
SI_tube_lenght <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SI") %>% summarise(mean=mean(tube_length_mm, na.rm = TRUE))
N_SI_tube_lenght <- sum(!is.na(floral_data$tube_length_mm))


# SC umbel angle degree
SC_umbel_angle <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SC") %>% summarise(mean=mean(umbel_angle_deg, na.rm = TRUE))
N_SC_umbel <- sum(!is.na(floral_data$umbel_angle_deg))

# SC flower number
SC_flower_number <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SC") %>% summarise(mean=mean(flower_number, na.rm = TRUE))
N_SC_flowernumber <- sum(!is.na(floral_data$flower_number))

# SC face diameter (mm)
SC_face_diameter <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SC") %>% summarise(mean=mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE))
N_SC_face_diameter <- sum(!is.na(floral_data$face_diam_mm))

# SC tube lenght (mm)
SC_tube_lenght <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SC") %>% summarise(mean=mean(tube_length_mm, na.rm = TRUE))
N_SC_tube_lenght <- sum(!is.na(floral_data$tube_length_mm))
```

Now make a second summary with one row per site: the site's mating system, its latitude, the number of plants and the mean face diameter. Then plot mean face diameter (y) against latitude (x), one point per site, coloured by mating system, with axis labels with units, a legend title and a clean theme.

```{r site-means}
site_means <- floral_data %>% group_by(site) %>% summarise(mean=mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), N_plants = n(), mating_sys  = first(self_inc), latitude_N = first(latitude_N))
```

```{r face-vs-latitude}
ggplot(aes(x=latitude_N, y=mean), data=dplyr::filter(site_means, !is.na(mating_sys))) + geom_point(aes(colour=mating_sys)) + scale_colour_manual(values = c("palevioletred1", "orchid4")) + labs(title="Face diameter vs Latitude", subtitle="mean face diameter (mm) of Nyctaginaceae against latitude",caption="Figure 3: shows mean face diameter (mm) of Nyctaginaceae against latitude in the sample, self-compatible in pink and self-incompatible in purple.") + xlab("Latitude") + ylab("Mean Face Diameter (mm)") + theme_classic()
```

