# Part 3 — From the sample to the population

## 6. Standard error and confidence interval

For `SI` and for `SC`, calculate the standard error of the mean and the 95% confidence interval **from the equations**: $SE = s / \sqrt{n}$ and $CI = \bar x \pm 1.96 \times SE$. Print both intervals.

```{r se-ci}
SI_SE_Conf <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SI") %>% summarise(n = sum(!is.na(face_diam_mm)), mean = mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SD = sd(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SE = SD / sqrt(n), CI_lower = mean - 1.96 * SE, CI_upper = mean + 1.96 * SE)
print(SI_SE_Conf)

SC_SE_Conf <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SC") %>% summarise(n = sum(!is.na(face_diam_mm)), mean = mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SD = sd(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SE = SD / sqrt(n), CI_lower = mean - 1.96 * SE, CI_upper = mean + 1.96 * SE)
print(SC_SE_Conf)
```

**Question 5:** What does each confidence interval represent? Do the two intervals overlap, and what does that suggest about the two populations?

**Answer:**

## 7. The central limit theorem

Set the seed with `set.seed(343)`. Use `sample()` to draw a random sub-sample of 10 plants from `SI`, then one of 100, then one of 500. For each sub-sample report the mean, the standard deviation and the width of the 95% confidence interval (upper limit minus lower limit), and compare them with the values for all 866 SI plants from Sections 4 and 6.

```{r clt}
set.seed(343)
SI_data <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SI")

# CHECK SLICE_SAMPLE ON CHEATSHEET!!!!

# For 10 plants
SI_10 <- SI_data %>% slice_sample(n=10)
data_10 <- SI_10 %>% summarise(n = sum(!is.na(face_diam_mm)), mean = mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SD = sd(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SE = SD / sqrt(n), CI_lower = mean - 1.96 * SE, CI_upper = mean + 1.96 * SE)
print(data_10)

# For 100 plants
SI_100 <- SI_data %>% slice_sample(n=100)
data_100 <- SI_100 %>% summarise(n = sum(!is.na(face_diam_mm)), mean = mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SD = sd(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SE = SD / sqrt(n), CI_lower = mean - 1.96 * SE, CI_upper = mean + 1.96 * SE)
print(data_100)

# For 500 plants
SI_500 <- SI_data %>% slice_sample(n=500)
data_500 <- SI_500 %>% summarise(n = sum(!is.na(face_diam_mm)), mean = mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SD = sd(face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE), SE = SD / sqrt(n), CI_lower = mean - 1.96 * SE, CI_upper = mean + 1.96 * SE)
print(data_500)
```

**Question 6:** As the sub-sample grows, which of the three numbers stays roughly where it is and which one shrinks? Explain why (hint: How does this relate to the standard error?)

**Answer:**

# Part 4 — Probability and tests

## 8. Z-scores and probability

Convert `SI` to z-scores and plot a histogram of them. Then convert the **mean of the SC plants** to a z-score relative to the SI sample (subtract the SI mean, divide by the SI standard deviation), and use `pnorm()` to calculate the probability of drawing a plant with a face diameter that small **or smaller** from the SI population.

```{r z-scores, message=FALSE}
# SI z-scores and plot a histogram of them
SI_data <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SI")

SI_mean <- mean(SI_data$face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE)
SI_sd <- sd(SI_data$face_diam_mm, na.rm = TRUE)
SI_z <- (SI_data$face_diam_mm - SI_mean)/SI_sd

ggplot(data.frame(SI_z), aes(x=SI_z)) + geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.25, fill = "lightpink", colour = "maroon") + labs(title="Histogram of sexual incompatible of Nyctaginaceae", x = "z-score", y = "Count") + theme_classic()

# Mean of SC 
SC_mean <- floral_data %>% filter(self_inc == "SC") %>% summarise(mean=mean(face_diam_mm, na.rm=TRUE)) %>% pull(mean)
SC_z <- (SC_mean - SI_mean)/SI_sd

```

**Question 7:** Is this a one-tailed or a two-tailed question, and why? What does the probability say about whether SC flowers are simply small SI flowers?

**Answer:**

